WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.560
Moderate impact is central to many FedRAMP conversations because many federal cloud

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.745
services process information or support use cases with meaningful sensitivity or mission

00:00:08.745 --> 00:00:09.370
importance.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:13.440
Moderate does not mean extreme, but it does mean the service needs stronger governance,

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.030
implementation discipline, testing, documentation,

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.750
and ongoing monitoring than a lower-impact scenario.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.960
It is often the practical middle ground for federal SaaS, platform,

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.446
and infrastructure offerings.

00:00:23.446 --> 00:00:26.800
A Moderate baseline expectation touches many operational themes:

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.610
identity and access control, audit logging, configuration management,

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:34.630
vulnerability handling, encryption, incident response, contingency planning,

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:37.515
risk assessment, vendor operations, and change management.

00:00:37.515 --> 00:00:40.640
The details depend on the architecture, but the pattern is consistent.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.240
The provider must be able to show not only that policies exist,

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:46.070
but that the system operates according to those policies.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.910
Moderate work affects engineering and operations directly.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:52.668
Engineers may need to design clearer boundaries, strengthen tenant separation,

00:00:52.668 --> 00:00:56.870
centralize logging, reduce manual configuration, document inherited controls,

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.370
and create repeatable evidence.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:01.560
Security teams need vulnerability workflows, monitoring routines,

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.745
incident response records, and risk tracking.

00:01:03.745 --> 00:01:07.280
Product teams need to understand what commitments are being made to agencies and what

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.080
future features may change scope.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.445
A common mistake is treating Moderate as paperwork.

00:01:11.445 --> 00:01:14.295
The package matters, but the package should reflect a real system.

00:01:14.295 --> 00:01:17.650
If the service cannot produce evidence, cannot explain responsibilities,

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.960
cannot track vulnerabilities, or cannot monitor meaningful changes,

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.960
the documentation will not hold up well.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.890
The baseline should become an operating model: protect the service, document honestly,

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:30.160
assess the controls, and keep monitoring after authorization.
